Xenith — Notice of Provenance
==============================

Xenith is licensed GPL-3.0 (see LICENSE). It started as a fork of Ascension
v1.2.6, then diverged. This file lists what carried over from that version;
README.md credits the wider plugin family it descends from.

Ascension v1.2.6 (GPL-3.0)
---------------------------
https://github.com/Servbot91/Sakotos-Stash-Repo/tree/main/plugins/Ascension

Carried over, with Xenith's own constants or mechanism changes noted where
they apply:

- The session repeat-opponent penalty and its three weight bands
  (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- The sigmoid form of the K-factor experience decay, including its midpoint
  and slope (`src/elo.js`'s `experienceFactor`). Xenith changes the asymptote
  and scales the endpoints to library size.
- The low-match boost and its three multiplier constants
  (`src/matchmaking.js`). Xenith changes the baseline calculation.
- The forced cross-tier match event, its trigger probability, and its
  minimum rating-gap threshold (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- The top-N weighted seed pool and its pool size (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- Recently-selected candidate tracking (`addToRecentlySelected` and
  `isRecentlySelected` in `src/matchmaking.js`). Xenith changes the buffer
  size and keeps one buffer per battle type.
- The opponent-selection failover order (anchor window, wider window,
  nearest rating, random) and the two-candidate minimum guard
  (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- The 15-point rating-gap anchor window for opponent selection
  (`src/matchmaking.js`).
- The rating-gap threshold where upset attenuation begins (`src/elo.js`).
  The curve past that threshold is Xenith's own.
- The `custom_fields` stats schema (`total_matches`, `wins`, `losses`,
  `draws`, `current_streak`, `best_streak`, `worst_streak`, `last_match`) and
  the legacy key names Xenith still reads as a migration fallback
  (`hotornot_stats`, `performer_record`; see `src/matchmaking.js` and
  `backend/tasks.py`'s `task_migrate`).
- The three match modes: a Swiss-style pairwise mode, a ladder-placement
  mode, and an incumbent-defense mode. Xenith's Gauntlet (`src/gauntlet.js`)
  and Champion (`src/champion.js`) are its own; the three-mode structure is
  not.
- About 40 CSS class names with the `hon-` prefix (`hon-modal*`, `hon-vs-*`,
  `hon-stats-*`, and others in `src/styles/xenith.css`). Eleven that were
  specific to Ascension's sidebar and action markup (`hon-plugin-layout`,
  `hon-main-plugin-content`, `hon-sidebar*`, `hon-action-btn`,
  `hon-battle-rank-badge-compact`) have since been renamed with a `xen-`
  prefix.

Independent work
-----------------
Written for Xenith, with no counterpart in v1.2.6:

- Entropy-weighted pairing priority (`src/elo.js`'s `entropy` and
  `priorityScore`, used throughout `src/matchmaking.js`).
- Library-scaled K-factor endpoints (`computeSystemConfig` in `src/elo.js`).
- The single symmetric upset attenuation.
- The uncertainty-discounted display rating.
- Monte-Carlo-calibrated tier bounds.
- Match-count-based cooldown buffers.
- The Bayesian Gauntlet placement engine (`src/gauntlet.js`).

`docs/rating-differences.md` compares Xenith's rating math against v1.2.6
function by function, with numbers.
